===Etymology===
{{circa|1600s}}, from earlier {{m|en|Tayuan}}, {{m|en|Tayoan}}, or other variants, from {{der|en|fos|taywan}} from {{m|fos|tayw||people}} + {{m|fos|an||place}}. Recorded in {{cog|nl|-}} as {{m|nl|Tayouan}} (and other variants), and in {{cog|nan|-}} as {{m|zh|大員|tr=Tāi-oân}} (and other variants).<ref>{{cite-web |last=Mair |first=V. H. |authorlink=Victor H. Mair |year=2010 |work=How to Forget Your Mother Tongue and Remember Your National Language |url=http://www.pinyin.info/readings/mair/taiwanese.html}}</ref> The term initially referred to a sandy peninsula in the area of modern-day {{w|Anping District}}, [[Tainan]], and eventually became the name of the entire island. See also {{zh-l|臺灣/台灣/台湾}}. Incorrectly understood (via the meaning of the Chinese characters) to mean 'terraced bay' and similar.

